T("Keyword-only parameters",
  "Put a bare `*` in the parameter list and every parameter after it must be passed **by name**. It stops confusing calls like `connect('db', 30, True)` where nobody can tell what 30 and True mean.",
  "def connect(host, *, timeout=10, retry=False):\n    return f'{host} timeout={timeout} retry={retry}'\n\nprint(connect('db', timeout=30))   # db timeout=30 retry=False\n# connect('db', 30)   would be a TypeError",
  "The `*` marks the end of the positional parameters. Python then refuses to match extra positional arguments to the names after it.",
  "Settings and flags in library functions; `sorted(items, reverse=True)` and `print(..., end='')` work this way."),
Q("With `def connect(host, *, timeout):`, which call works?", ["connect('x', timeout=5)", "connect('x', 5)", "connect(timeout=5)"], "connect('x', timeout=5)",
  "`timeout` comes after `*`, so it must be named. `host` is still required, by position or name.",
  feedback={"connect('x', 5)": "After the `*`, parameters can't be filled by position, so 5 has nowhere to go: TypeError."}),
T("Unpacking arguments with * and **",
  "In a **call**, `*` spreads a list or tuple into separate positional arguments, and `**` spreads a dictionary into keyword arguments. It's the reverse of `*args` and `**kwargs` in a definition.",
  "def volume(l, w, h):\n    return l * w * h\n\ndims = [2, 3, 4]\nprint(volume(*dims))           # 24\n\nsize = {'l': 1, 'w': 2, 'h': 5}\nprint(volume(**size))          # 10\nprint(*['a', 'b', 'c'])        # a b c",
  "`f(*[2, 3, 4])` becomes `f(2, 3, 4)`. `f(**{'l': 1})` becomes `f(l=1)`. The keys must match parameter names.",
  "Passing data you already have in a list or dict to a function, and forwarding arguments from one function to another."),
C("Spread a list into a function",
  "Call `volume` with the three numbers in `dims` using `*`. It should print: 24",
  "def volume(l, w, h):\n    return l * w * h\n\ndims = [2, 3, 4]\n# TODO: print the volume using *dims\n",
  "def volume(l, w, h):\n    return l * w * h\n\ndims = [2, 3, 4]\nprint(volume(*dims))",
  "print(volume(*dims))",
  [("* in a call spreads", "`f(*items)` passes items[0], items[1], ... as separate arguments. The list needs exactly as many items as the function has parameters.",
    "def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add(*[3, 4]))  # 7"),
   ("Without the star", "`volume(dims)` would pass the whole list as `l` and leave `w` and `h` missing: TypeError. Add the `*` in front of dims.",
    "print(max(*[4, 9, 2]))  # 9")]),
T("Sort keys that return tuples",
  "`max`, `min` and `sorted` all take `key=`. If the key returns a **tuple**, items are compared by its first element, then the second to break ties, and so on. Negate a number in the key to sort that part from high to low.",
  "people = [('Ann', 30), ('Bo', 25), ('Cy', 30)]\nprint(max(people, key=lambda p: p[1]))       # ('Ann', 30)\n# age high to low, then name A to Z\nprint(sorted(people, key=lambda p: (-p[1], p[0])))\n# [('Ann', 30), ('Cy', 30), ('Bo', 25)]",
  "Tuples compare element by element, so a tuple key gives a multi-level sort. `-p[1]` makes larger ages smaller, so they come first in ascending order.",
  "Leaderboards (score high to low, then name), sorting records by several columns, and picking the best match by several criteria."),
